= [+ date_label +] — The pollster adds another party

The first change appears in university districts, high-rent neighbourhoods
and households with a trade union member under forty. The interviews describe
the same route into politics: a dormitory or workplace campaign, then a branch
meeting, then the answer **[+ red_renaissance_identity +]** when the pollster
asks for a party.

[? if red_renaissance_protest_turnout > 0: The national march settled the
question of visibility. A voter no longer has to explain what the party is;
they can point to the crowd outside the Chancellery and the government's answer
to **[+ red_renaissance_protest_demand +]**.?]
[? if red_renaissance_protest_turnout = 0: We are not in government, so the
new party cannot put our ministers at the head of its grievance. It instead
compares our parliamentary programme with the ownership, housing and workplace
demands we decline to make.?]

[? if red_renaissance_identity = "Razem": Razem now polls **[+ razem_poll
+]%** on its own line. Every tenth is drawn from a Left family that once shared
one committee.?]
[? if red_renaissance_identity = "Akcja Socjalistyczna": Akcja
Socjalistyczna now polls **[+ tak_rozwoj_poll +]%**. Its five-percent threshold
is a danger to it and a weapon against us: every warning about a wasted vote
also publicises the choice.?]

- @red_voters_ledger
- @red_voters_debate
- @red_voters_copy

